❓ Day 2 Interview-Style Questions
Q1. Retrieve all employees whose salary is greater than 50,000.

✅ Solution

SELECT name, salary 
FROM Employee
WHERE salary > 50000;

Q2. Find employees whose name starts with A.

✅ Solution

SELECT * 
FROM Employee
WHERE name LIKE 'A%';

Q3. List employees between age 25 and 30.

✅ Solution

SELECT * 
FROM Employee
WHERE age BETWEEN 25 AND 30;

Q4. Show unique department IDs from the Employee table.

✅ Solution

SELECT DISTINCT dept_id FROM Employee;

Q5. Retrieve top 3 highest-paid employees.

✅ Solution (MySQL/Postgres)

SELECT * 
FROM Employee
ORDER BY salary DESC
LIMIT 3;


✅ Solution (SQL Server)

SELECT TOP 3 * 
FROM Employee
ORDER BY salary DESC;

Q6. Find employees hired after 2020 and sort them by hire_date (newest first).

✅ Solution

SELECT name, hire_date 
FROM Employee
WHERE hire_date > '2020-01-01'
ORDER BY hire_date DESC;

Q7. Retrieve employees who belong to department 101 or 103.

✅ Solution

SELECT * 
FROM Employee
WHERE dept_id IN (101, 103);

Q8. Find the second highest salary (common interview question).

✅ Solution (Subquery)

SELECT MAX(salary) 
FROM Employee
WHERE salary < (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM Employee);


✅ Solution (LIMIT + OFFSET – MySQL/Postgres)

SELECT salary 
FROM Employee
ORDER BY salary DESC
LIMIT 1 OFFSET 1;

Q9. Show employees whose salary is not equal to 45,000.

✅ Solution

SELECT * 
FROM Employee
WHERE salary <> 45000;

Q10. Retrieve employees and sort them by age ascending and then salary descending.

✅ Solution

SELECT * 
FROM Employee
ORDER BY age ASC, salary DESC;
